
| Number | Stream | Meaning       |
| ------ | ------ | ------------- |
| `0`    | stdin  | Input         |
| `1`    | stdout | Normal output |
| `2`    | stderr | Error output  |


Standard input — stdin
======================
Normally, a command takes input from the keyboard.

cat < input.txt --> Here, input.txt becomes the input to cat.


Standard output — stdout
========================
Normally, command output appears on the terminal.

ls > files.txt  --> Use > to redirect output to a file. If files.txt already exists, > overwrites it.
ls >> files.txt --> It does not overwrite the existing content.


Standard error — stderr
=======================
Errors normally appear on the terminal.

ls /wrongfolder 2> error.txt  --> Redirect errors using 2> . The error message goes into error.txt.



Redirect output and errors separately
=====================================
command > output.txt 2> error.txt

Normal output → output.txt
Errors → error.txt


Redirect both output and errors to the same file
================================================
command > output.txt 2>&1     or     command &> output.txt

> → send stdout to output.txt
2>&1 → send stderr to the same place as stdout


Pipe |
======
A pipe sends the output of one command directly as input to another command. 

cat file.txt | grep "error"
